[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Read-Write Transactions and Optimistic Concurrency Control in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building concurrent applications, managing data consistency is critical. Firebase Firestore handles concurrency using Read-Write Transactions and Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC). This tutorial explains how transactions work under the hood and how to implement them safely in your code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Firestore Transactions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A transaction is a set of read and write operations on one or more documents. Transactions guarantee that either all operations complete successfully as a single atomic batch, or none of them apply. Firestore transactions follow ACID properties for isolation and atomicity."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"All reads within a transaction must happen before any writes."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Transactions are retried automatically by the SDK if contention occurs."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The maximum duration for a single transaction is 60 seconds."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike pessimistic locking (which locks rows or documents until an operation finishes), Firestore uses Optimistic Concurrency Control. OCC assumes that multiple transactions can complete without interfering with each other."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"During a transaction, Firestore reads documents without setting locks. When you attempt to commit the writes, Firestore checks if any of the read documents were modified by another transaction in the meantime. If a conflict is detected, the current transaction fails and is automatically retried by the client library."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Implementation: Running a Transaction"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following JavaScript example demonstrates how to implement a safe read-write transaction to increment a counter document."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const { initializeApp } = require('firebase/app');
const { getFirestore, runTransaction, doc } = require('firebase/firestore');

const db = getFirestore();

async function incrementCounter(docRef) {
  try {
    await runTransaction(db, async (transaction) => {
      const sfDoc = await transaction.get(docRef);
      if (!sfDoc.exists()) {
        throw new Error('Document does not exist!');
      }

      const newPopulation = sfDoc.data().population + 1;
      transaction.update(docRef, { population: newPopulation });
    });
    console.log('Transaction successfully committed!');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Transaction failed: ', e);
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Firestore Transactions"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep transactions short and touch only the documents you need to minimize contention."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never perform non-idempotent operations (like sending an email or calling a third-party API) directly inside the transaction callback, because the callback may execute multiple times due to retries."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always place read operations before write operations within the transaction block."}]
